ution time rather than preventing it at the sizing layer itself. That is a reasonable and consistent design choice (mirrors the disclosed-not-corrected pattern already used for manual_offbook_assets_vnd and the dividend double-count), and both gates are confirmed live elsewhere in this codebase — but it does mean the sizing/execution split now leans entirely on those two gates continuing to work correctly; if either regresses, this NAV fix would silently oversize plans rather than fail loudly on its own.", "recommended_reruns": ["After the fix goes live [REDACTED], spot-check one real cron-produced active_nav_[REDACTED].json against a same-timestamp DNSE balances() call to confirm live (non-selfcheck) behavior matches the selfcheck's synthetic payloads", "Confirm check_plan_funding() and L2 JIT-unpark actually fire (not just exist) on the first live session with a large unsettled-cash active_nav gap, to close out the killer_objection empirically rather than by code-reading alone", "Re-run compute_active_nav_selfcheck.py after the eventual commit to this nested mike/ repo, to catch any last-minute working-tree drift before it lands"], "summary": "The claim is well-supported: the code change in mike/bin/compute_active_nav.py (currently uncommitted in the nested mike/ git repo, verified via git diff) matches the report's description exactly — cash basis moved from DNSEBroker.get_cash() (availableCash) to a new cash_basis() function returning totalCash−totalDebt, with three fail-closed guards reused (not duplicated) from park_holdings.py and a hard sys.exit(4) placed before any file write. I independently re-ran the cited selfcheck (26/26 PASS) under three different environments including two adversarial TZ settings, and independently reproduced the report's headline [REDACTED] NAV number (961,311,265) by reading nav_history_[REDACTED].csv, which is written by a genuinely different script/pathway (daily_nav_snapshot.py) — an exact match to the VND. I also independently 